The Security Council,

Reaffirming its primary responsibility under the Charter of the United Nations for the maintenance of international peace and security,

Reaffirming the Purposes and Principles of the Charter of the United Nations, and recalling the obligation of all Member States under Article 2 of the Charter to settle their international disputes by peaceful means and to refrain from the threat or use of force,

Expressing grave concern over the continuing loss of life, civilian suffering, and destruction resulting from the conflict in and around Ukraine, as well as the broader risks to regional and international security,

Emphasizing the vital importance of protecting civilians, critical civilian infrastructure, and ensuring the safety and security of nuclear facilities in accordance with international humanitarian law,

Noting with appreciation ongoing diplomatic engagements, mediation, and dialogue initiatives undertaken by Member States, including recent high-level discussions aimed at ending the war,

Convinced of the urgent need for a negotiated, durable, and just political settlement to the conflict,

1. Calls for an immediate reduction in violence and urges the parties to work expeditiously toward a comprehensive and sustainable cessation of hostilities;

2. Calls upon the parties to engage constructively and in good faith in direct negotiations to achieve a peaceful, just, and lasting resolution to the conflict, in accordance with the Principles of the Charter of the United Nations;

3. Welcomes all diplomatic efforts, good offices, and initiatives facilitated by Member States to advance dialogue, de-escalation, and an enduring framework for peace;

4. Demands full compliance by all parties with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including the protection of civilian populations, critical infrastructure, and the facilitation of full, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access;

5. Reiterates the critical necessity of ensuring the physical integrity, safety, and security of all nuclear facilities in the conflict zone, and supports the continued monitoring role of the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA);

6. Requests the Secretary-General to lend his good offices in support of peace initiatives and to report to the Security Council as appropriate on the progress of diplomatic efforts and the implementation of this resolution;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Reaffirming its primary responsibility under the Charter of the United Nations for the maintenance of international peace and security,

Reaffirming the Purposes and Principles of the Charter of the United Nations, and recalling the obligation of all Member States under Article 2 of the Charter to settle their international disputes by peaceful means and to refrain from the threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or political independence of any State,

Reaffirming its commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity, and territorial integrity of Ukraine within its internationally recognized borders, extending to its territorial waters,

Expressing grave concern over the continuing loss of life, civilian suffering, and destruction resulting from the conflict in and around Ukraine, as well as the broader risks to regional and international security, including the adverse impacts on global food and energy security, particularly affecting developing countries,

Emphasizing the vital importance of protecting civilians, critical civilian infrastructure, and ensuring the safety and security of nuclear facilities in accordance with international humanitarian law,

Noting with appreciation ongoing diplomatic engagements, mediation, and dialogue initiatives undertaken by Member States, including recent high-level discussions aimed at ending the war,

Convinced of the urgent need for a negotiated, durable, and just political settlement to the conflict that addresses its root causes and takes into account the legitimate security concerns of all parties,

1. Calls for an immediate reduction in violence and urges the parties to work expeditiously toward a comprehensive, durable, and sustainable cessation of hostilities;

2. Calls upon the parties to engage constructively and in good faith in direct negotiations to achieve a peaceful, just, and lasting resolution to the conflict, in accordance with the Purposes and Principles of the Charter of the United Nations in their entirety and interconnectedness, notably the principles of sovereign equality, the non-use of force, and respect for sovereignty and territorial integrity, taking into account the legitimate security concerns of all parties and addressing the root causes of the conflict;

3. Welcomes all diplomatic efforts, good offices, and initiatives facilitated by Member States to advance dialogue, de-escalation, and an enduring framework for peace;

4. Demands full compliance by all parties with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including the protection of civilian populations, critical infrastructure, and the facilitation of full, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access, and emphasizes the importance of accountability for violations of international humanitarian law;

5. Reiterates the critical necessity of ensuring the physical integrity, safety, and security of all nuclear facilities in the conflict zone, and supports the continued monitoring role of the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA);

6. Requests the Secretary-General to lend his good offices in support of peace initiatives, in consultation with the parties, and to report to the Security Council as appropriate on the progress of diplomatic efforts and the implementation of this resolution;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

